<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera.any" android:required="true" />

    <!--
      Both are merged in by dependencies (MediaPipe's optional model downloader and CameraX). This
      app has no business asking for either: the model bundles ship in assets/ and nothing here
      talks to a network. It points a camera at a child, so an unexplained INTERNET permission is
      not a cosmetic issue — it is the difference between a claim that footage stays on the device
      and a claim that it could not leave even if something tried.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" tools:node="remove" />

    <application
        android:label="remapy"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Remapy">
        <!--
          `screenOrientation` is the *initial* framing only; the overlay's toggle overrides it at
          runtime via `requestedOrientation`. Portrait is the default: it is the orientation a
          phone is picked up in, it frames a standing or crawling child better than a wide view of
          the floor either side of him, and it is how the app is actually reached for mid-session.
          The landscape toggle is still there for a tripod.

          **This value and `MainActivity.portrait` must agree, and nothing reconciles them at
          runtime.** Change one without the other and the window is tall while the flag says
          landscape: the resolution request goes out transposed, the overlay button reads the wrong
          word, and the operator's first tap is spent resyncing the two rather than changing
          anything.

          `configChanges` is load-bearing rather than incidental: it stops the activity being
          recreated on rotation, which would tear down and rebuild the MediaPipe tasks — the exact
          native use-after-free that `PosePipeline.reset`'s doc describes. MainActivity overrides
          `onConfigurationChanged` and rebinds CameraX itself instead. Do not remove one without
          the other.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:screenOrientation="portrait"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|keyboardHidden"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Remapy">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
    </application>

</manifest>
